+# Forking Base in this checkout
+
+Short version: `yarn fork base` does **not** fork Base. It forks Ethereum
+mainnet, and `cast chain-id` cannot tell you the difference because the chain id
+is hardcoded. The USDC reads are correct — there is no contract at
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` on the chain the node is actually
+serving.
+
+## What the onboarding note gets wrong
+
+### 1. The `base` argument is silently dropped
+
+`packages/foundry/package.json:15`:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+That `${1:-mainnet}` is written as if yarn bound script arguments to shell
+positionals. Yarn 4 (`packageManager: yarn@4.13.0`) does not: `$1` is never set,
+so the default branch always wins and `FORK_URL` is always `mainnet`. Worse,
+because the script text references a positional, yarn also stops appending the
+extra argument — so the word `base` vanishes entirely rather than producing an
+error.
+
+Observed in this checkout (`make` echoes the recipe before running it):
+
+```
+$ yarn fork base
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337
+Error: failed to get fork block number
+- Error #0: error sending request for url (https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/...)
+```
+
+`mainnet` is an alias resolved out of `[rpc_endpoints]` in
+`packages/foundry/foundry.toml:14`:
+
+```toml
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+```
+
+So the node people got was an Ethereum mainnet fork. On Ethereum mainnet,
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` is an empty account — `eth_getCode`
+returns `0x`, every `staticcall` into it returns empty returndata, and decoding
+that as `uint256`/`string` gives you zeros or a revert. Nothing is wrong with the
+contract; it is calling an address that holds no code.
+
+Passing the value through the yarn script does not help either —
+`yarn fork FORK_URL=base` also runs `anvil --fork-url mainnet`, because the
+argument is dropped for the same reason.
+
+### 2. `cast chain-id` proves nothing
+
+`packages/foundry/Makefile:15-16`:
+
+```make
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+The chain id is **pinned to 31337 regardless of what is being forked** (that is
+deliberate — `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:20-22` targets
+`chains.foundry`, i.e. 31337, so the frontend keeps working). A fork of Base, a
+fork of Ethereum mainnet, a fork of Optimism, and a bare `yarn chain` with no
+fork at all all answer `31337`. "I got a number back" only proves something is
+listening on 8545.
+
+### 3. There may not even be a fork on 8545
+
+Right now, in this checkout, port 8545 is held by a **plain (unforked) Hardhat
+node from an unrelated project** — not anvil:
+
+```
+$ lsof -nP -iTCP:8545 -sTCP:LISTEN
+node ... hardhat/dist/src/cli.js node --network hardhat
+$ cast client --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+HardhatNetwork/3.15.0/@nomicfoundation/edr/0.3.8
+$ cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+31337
+```
+
+It answers RPC, it mines transactions, it reports 31337 — and it has no tokens on
+it whatsoever. Because it holds the port, `yarn fork ...` dies with
+`Error: Address already in use (os error 48)`; if you start the fork in another
+terminal and do not read its output, you will keep talking to the stale node and
+see exactly the reported symptom. Kill whatever owns 8545 before forking.
+
+## The command that actually forks Base here
+
+Bypass the broken yarn wrapper and drive the Makefile target directly, from
+`packages/foundry` (the alias lookup needs `foundry.toml` in scope):
+
+```bash
+cd packages/foundry
+make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+or, from the repo root:
+
+```bash
+yarn workspace @se-2/foundry exec make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+Either one runs `anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337`, and anvil resolves
+`base` against `packages/foundry/foundry.toml:26`:
+
+```toml
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+Any explicit URL works in place of the alias, e.g.
+`make fork FORK_URL=https://mainnet.base.org` or your own Base RPC — recommended,
+since `mainnet.base.org` is rate-limited and archive-state reads at a pinned
+block will be flaky against it.
+
+Worth fixing at the source: `packages/foundry/package.json:15` should be
+`"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=$FORK_URL"` (with a Makefile default such as
+`FORK_URL ?= mainnet`) so that `FORK_URL=base yarn fork` works and a typo'd
+network name fails loudly instead of quietly falling back to Ethereum.
+
+## The check that would have caught this
+
+Do not ask the node *what chain id it claims*. Ask it for **state that only Base
+has**. This is the whole check:
+
+```bash
+RPC=http://127.0.0.1:8545
+
+# Must print USDC / 6 / a nonzero supply. Empty output or a revert => not Base.
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "symbol()(string)"   --rpc-url $RPC
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "decimals()(uint8)"  --rpc-url $RPC
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "totalSupply()(uint256)" --rpc-url $RPC
+
+# Negative control: Ethereum-mainnet USDC. Must be 0x here.
+# If THIS one has code, you are on a mainnet fork, not Base.
+cast code 0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48 --rpc-url $RPC
+
+# OP-Stack predeploy: present on Base (and other OP chains), absent on L1.
+cast call 0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000015 "number()(uint256)" --rpc-url $RPC
+```
+
+The positive probe alone is enough to unblock you; the negative control is what
+turns "the token reads empty" into "you are on the wrong chain" instead of "our
+contract is broken". A `symbol()` that answers `USDC` cannot be produced by a
+bare devnet or by an Ethereum fork.
+
+Two cheap extras, both of which the chain-id check misses:
+
+```bash
+# Is this even anvil? A Hardhat node answering 31337 is the classic decoy.
+cast client --rpc-url $RPC
+
+# anvil-only: reports the URL and block actually being forked.
+cast rpc anvil_nodeInfo --rpc-url $RPC | jq '.forkConfig'
+```
+
+`anvil_nodeInfo` is the most direct answer when the node *is* anvil — it names
+the fork URL outright — but it errors with `Method anvil_nodeInfo is not
+supported` on a Hardhat node, which is itself a useful signal. The `cast call`
+probes above are the portable version and are what belongs in the onboarding
+note.
+
+Rule of thumb for this repo: **chain id 31337 is a constant, not evidence.**
+Verify a fork by reading a contract that only the forked chain has.

